To control for the training set size asymmetry between \cls{} (${\sim}$70K instances) and \traj{} (386 variant-level instances, ${\sim}$8K per-test equivalents, at repo level), we downsample \cls{} training data to match \traj{} in data volume. Downsampled \cls{} (Qwen3-4B, seed 42) achieves 80.74\% repo-level per-test accuracy, only 2.89\pp{} below the full-data \cls{} (83.63\%) and still 7.82\pp{} above \traj{} (72.92\%). The \cls{} formulation advantage is not attributable to training data volume.

At function level, the data volume relationship is reversed: \traj{} uses 8,052 variant-level instances covering 2.24$\times$ more unique code variants than \cls{}/\cwm{} (3,596 unique variants from 15,290 per-test instances). \cls{} nonetheless outperforms \traj{} by 2.13\pp{} (88.43\% vs.\ 86.30\%, Qwen3-4B), ruling out data volume as an explanation for the function-level gap. An extreme downsample ablation (CLS trained on only 447 per-test instances, ${\sim}$105 unique variants) achieves 78.57 $\pm$ 0.78\%, confirming that per-test classification maintains reasonable performance even under severe data reduction, though it falls below \traj{} (86.30\%) given the 20$\times$ data disadvantage.

